# §1. Tax imposed

> Module Section1

> Using SaraWorld as W
> Using SaraKinship as K
> Using SaraPredicates as P
> Using SaraStatus as St
> Using Section7703 as S7703
> Using Section152 as S152
> Using Section63 as S63

Port of `section1.pl`. §1 imposes tax on taxable income according to one of four
rate schedules, selected by filing status:

| schedule | §1 | who |
|---|---|---|
| A | (a) | married filing jointly, and surviving spouses |
| B | (b) | heads of household |
| C | (c) | unmarried, not a surviving spouse or head of household |
| D | (d) | married filing separately |

Each schedule is five brackets. SARA exposes every bracket as its own predicate
(`s1_a_i/2` … `s1_d_v/2`), and 60 of the 376 cases query one directly, asking
whether a *claimed* tax figure is the one the bracket produces for a given
taxable income. Both uses are served here from one table, so the twenty bracket
predicates are one definition rather than twenty.

```catala-metadata
declaration enumeration Schedule:
  -- ScheduleA
  -- ScheduleB
  -- ScheduleC
  -- ScheduleD

## One row of a rate schedule: tax is `base` plus `rate` of the excess over
## `floor`, provided income does not exceed `ceiling`. The top bracket has no
## ceiling.
declaration structure Bracket:
  data floor content money
  data ceiling content optional of money
  data base content money
  data rate content decimal
```

## Rate schedules

The figures are the 1994 tables SARA encodes, transcribed from the statute text
in `section1.pl`.

```catala-metadata
declaration brackets_of content list of Bracket
  depends on sched content Schedule
  equals
    match sched with pattern
    -- ScheduleA: [
         Bracket { -- floor: $0      -- ceiling: Present content $36,900
                   -- base: $0          -- rate: 0.15 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $36,900 -- ceiling: Present content $89,150
                   -- base: $5,535      -- rate: 0.28 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $89,150 -- ceiling: Present content $140,000
                   -- base: $20,165     -- rate: 0.31 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $140,000 -- ceiling: Present content $250,000
                   -- base: $35,928.50  -- rate: 0.36 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $250,000 -- ceiling: Absent
                   -- base: $75,528.50  -- rate: 0.396 } ]
    -- ScheduleB: [
         Bracket { -- floor: $0      -- ceiling: Present content $29,600
                   -- base: $0          -- rate: 0.15 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $29,600 -- ceiling: Present content $76,400
                   -- base: $4,440      -- rate: 0.28 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $76,400 -- ceiling: Present content $127,500
                   -- base: $17,544     -- rate: 0.31 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $127,500 -- ceiling: Present content $250,000
                   -- base: $33,385     -- rate: 0.36 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $250,000 -- ceiling: Absent
                   -- base: $77,485     -- rate: 0.396 } ]
    -- ScheduleC: [
         Bracket { -- floor: $0      -- ceiling: Present content $22,100
                   -- base: $0          -- rate: 0.15 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $22,100 -- ceiling: Present content $53,500
                   -- base: $3,315      -- rate: 0.28 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $53,500 -- ceiling: Present content $115,000
                   -- base: $12,107     -- rate: 0.31 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $115,000 -- ceiling: Present content $250,000
                   -- base: $31,172     -- rate: 0.36 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $250,000 -- ceiling: Absent
                   -- base: $79,772     -- rate: 0.396 } ]
    -- ScheduleD: [
         Bracket { -- floor: $0      -- ceiling: Present content $18,450
                   -- base: $0          -- rate: 0.15 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $18,450 -- ceiling: Present content $44,575
                   -- base: $2,767.50   -- rate: 0.28 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $44,575 -- ceiling: Present content $70,000
                   -- base: $10,082.50  -- rate: 0.31 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $70,000 -- ceiling: Present content $125,000
                   -- base: $17,964.25  -- rate: 0.36 };
         Bracket { -- floor: $125,000 -- ceiling: Absent
                   -- base: $37,764.25  -- rate: 0.396 } ]
```

## Bracket arithmetic

The Prolog computes `round(base + (Taxinc - floor) * rate)` in floating point
and rounds to a whole dollar. Catala works in exact decimal, so the port rounds
the same way but without the float error; `sara_scripts/DEVIATIONS.md` records the
comparison once the differential runner has checked all 60 §1 cases.

```catala-metadata
declaration bracket_tax content money
  depends on b content Bracket, taxable_income content money
  equals
    money of (round of (decimal of (b.base + (taxable_income - b.floor) * b.rate)))

## Does this bracket govern the given taxable income? The Prolog writes the
## test as `Taxinc =< ceiling, floor < Taxinc`, so the floor is exclusive and
## the ceiling inclusive. The first bracket has floor $0 and the Prolog omits
## its lower test entirely, which matters only for negative income; income is
## never negative in the corpus.
declaration bracket_applies content boolean
  depends on b content Bracket, taxable_income content money
  equals
    (b.floor = $0 or b.floor < taxable_income)
    and (match b.ceiling with pattern
         -- Absent: true
         -- Present content c: taxable_income <= c)
```

## The twenty bracket predicates

`s1_a_i/2` … `s1_d_v/2`. A case asks whether a claimed tax figure is what the
bracket yields, which holds only when the bracket applies *and* the arithmetic
agrees — exactly the Prolog's conjunction.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope BracketHolds:
  input sched content Schedule
  ## 1-based, matching the statute's (i)…(v).
  input index content integer
  input taxable_income content money
  input claimed_tax content money
  output holds content boolean
```

```catala
scope BracketHolds:
  definition holds equals
    match (List.nth_element of (brackets_of of sched), index) with pattern
    -- Absent: false
    -- Present content b:
        (bracket_applies of b, taxable_income)
        and (bracket_tax of b, taxable_income) = claimed_tax
```

## Tax under a schedule

`s1_a_tax/2` and its siblings: the tax for a taxable income under one schedule,
being whichever bracket applies.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope ScheduleTax:
  input sched content Schedule
  input taxable_income content money
  output tax content money
```

```catala
scope ScheduleTax:
  definition tax equals
    match
      (List.first_element of
        (list of b among (brackets_of of sched)
         such that bracket_applies of b, taxable_income))
    with pattern
    -- Absent: $0
    -- Present content b: bracket_tax of b, taxable_income
```

## The subsections themselves

`s1_a/4`, `s1_b/4`, `s1_c/4` and `s1_d/5` each assert that the taxpayer falls
under that subsection *and* that the claimed tax is what the schedule yields for
their taxable income. Taxable income comes from §63, which the cases supply by
injection; where it is absent the subsection cannot hold.

```catala-metadata
declaration schedule_of content Schedule
  depends on st content St.Status
  equals
    match st with pattern
    -- JointOrSurvivingSpouse: Schedule.ScheduleA
    -- HeadOfHousehold: Schedule.ScheduleB
    -- Unmarried: Schedule.ScheduleC
    -- MarriedFilingSeparately: Schedule.ScheduleD

declaration scope SubsectionHolds:
  input status content St.Status
  input subsection content Schedule
  ## §63 taxable income, as injected by the case.
  input taxable_income content optional of money
  ## ... and as the statute derives it, with its own availability: a *replaced*
  ## `s63/3` leaves only the granted rows. Both are solutions of the same
  ## `s63(Taxp,Taxy,Taxinc)` goal, so either may satisfy the claimed tax.
  input derived_income content money
  input derived_applies content boolean
  input claimed_tax content money
  output holds content boolean
```

```catala
scope SubsectionHolds:
  definition holds equals
    (schedule_of of status) = subsection
    and ((match taxable_income with pattern
          -- Absent: false
          -- Present content ti:
              (output of ScheduleTax with {
                 -- sched: subsection -- taxable_income: ti }).tax = claimed_tax)
         or (derived_applies
             and (output of ScheduleTax with {
                    -- sched: subsection
                    -- taxable_income: derived_income }).tax = claimed_tax))
```

## §1 as a value

`s1/4` is what `tax/3` asks for, and it wants a *number*, not a yes/no. The
four subsections are tried in the order the Prolog writes them — (a), then (b),
then (c), then (d) — and the first that applies fixes the schedule. They are
not quite mutually exclusive: a surviving spouse who also heads a household
satisfies both (a) and (b), and Prolog's first solution is (a).

`SaraStatus.FilingStatus` cannot stand in for this. It reads §7703(a) rather
than the whole of §7703, and it has no equivalent of the nonresident-alien bar
that (a)(1) puts on filing jointly. Both matter here, where the answer is a
dollar figure rather than a status.

Taxable income is derived from §63 rather than read from the case's grant. That
is the Prolog's own order: a case that also asserts `s63/3` adds a *clause*
after the statute's, so the derived value is the first solution, and `tax/3`
takes the first solution.

```catala-metadata
declaration scope Tax:
  input world content W.World
  input injections content list of W.Injection
  input taxpayer content W.Entity
  input taxable_year content integer
  input child_of content list of K.Kinship
  input sibling_of content list of K.Kinship
  input descendent_of content list of K.Kinship
  input stepsibling_of content list of K.Kinship
  input stepparent_of content list of K.Kinship
  input child_in_law_of content list of K.Kinship
  input parent_in_law_of content list of K.Kinship
  input sibling_in_law_of content list of K.Kinship
  ## The §7703 spouses, which (a)(1) and (d) both quantify over.
  output spouses content list of W.Entity
  output taxable_income_applies content boolean
  output married content boolean
  ## s1_a_1/6 — married, filing jointly, and neither spouse a nonresident alien
  output holds_a_1 content boolean
  ## s1_a/4
  output holds_a content boolean
  ## s1_b/4
  output holds_b content boolean
  ## s1_c/4
  output holds_c content boolean
  ## s1_d/5
  output holds_d content boolean
  ## s63/3
  output taxable_income content money
  output subsection content optional of Schedule
  ## s1/4 — the tax, and whether the goal has a solution at all
  output tax content money
  output holds content boolean
```

```catala
scope Tax:
  definition spouses equals
    (output of S7703.MaritalStatus with {
       -- injections: injections -- world: world -- taxpayer: taxpayer
       -- taxable_year: taxable_year
       -- qualifying_dependants:
            (output of S152.QualifyingChildrenOf with {
               -- injections: injections -- world: world
               -- taxpayer: taxpayer -- taxable_year: taxable_year
               -- child_of: child_of -- sibling_of: sibling_of
               -- descendent_of: descendent_of
               -- stepsibling_of: stepsibling_of }).children }).spouses

  definition married equals
    (number of spouses) > 0
    or (W.injected_holds of injections, P.pred_s7703_4,
          [taxpayer.id; -1; -1; W.num_key of taxable_year])

  ## A joint return by these two spanning the whole taxable year, which the
  ## Prolog states by unifying its start and end with the year boundaries.
  definition holds_a_1 equals
    exists sp among spouses such that
      (exists jr among world.events such that
         jr.kinds contains W.EventKind.JointReturn
         and jr.agents contains taxpayer and jr.agents contains sp
         and jr.starts contains (W.first_day_year of taxable_year)
         and jr.ends contains (W.last_day_year of taxable_year))
      # nonresident aliens cannot file jointly
      and not (exists nra among world.events such that
                 nra.kinds contains W.EventKind.NonresidentAlien
                 and (nra.agents contains taxpayer or nra.agents contains sp)
                 and ((number of nra.starts) = 0
                      or (exists st among nra.starts such that
                            st <= (W.last_day_year of taxable_year)))
                 and ((number of nra.ends) = 0
                      or (exists en among nra.ends such that
                            (W.first_day_year of taxable_year) <= en)))

  definition holds_a equals
    holds_a_1
    or (output of St.FilingStatus with {
          -- world: world -- injections: injections -- taxpayer: taxpayer
          -- taxable_year: taxable_year }).surviving_spouse

  definition holds_b equals
    (output of St.FilingStatus with {
       -- world: world -- injections: injections -- taxpayer: taxpayer
       -- taxable_year: taxable_year }).head_of_household

  definition holds_c equals
    not (output of St.FilingStatus with {
           -- world: world -- injections: injections -- taxpayer: taxpayer
           -- taxable_year: taxable_year }).surviving_spouse
    and not holds_b
    and not married

  ## (d) is per spouse: married to somebody they did not file jointly with.
  definition holds_d equals
    exists sp among spouses such that
      not (exists jr among world.events such that
             jr.kinds contains W.EventKind.JointReturn
             and jr.agents contains taxpayer and jr.agents contains sp
             and jr.starts contains (W.first_day_year of taxable_year)
             and jr.ends contains (W.last_day_year of taxable_year))

  definition taxable_income equals
    (output of S63.TaxableIncome with {
       -- world: world -- injections: injections -- taxpayer: taxpayer
       -- taxable_year: taxable_year
       -- child_of: child_of -- sibling_of: sibling_of
       -- descendent_of: descendent_of -- stepsibling_of: stepsibling_of
       -- stepparent_of: stepparent_of
       -- child_in_law_of: child_in_law_of
       -- parent_in_law_of: parent_in_law_of
       -- sibling_in_law_of: sibling_in_law_of }).income

  ## `s1/4` reaches its rate schedule through §63, and §63 through §151. When a
  ## case *replaces* `s151/5` and names nobody relevant, the chain fails rather
  ## than yielding a taxable income of zero, and `tax(Taxp,Taxy,T)` then takes
  ## its `\+ s1(...)` branch with `Income_tax is 0`. See D39.
  definition taxable_income_applies equals
    (output of S63.TaxableIncome with {
       -- world: world -- injections: injections -- taxpayer: taxpayer
       -- taxable_year: taxable_year
       -- child_of: child_of -- sibling_of: sibling_of
       -- descendent_of: descendent_of -- stepsibling_of: stepsibling_of
       -- stepparent_of: stepparent_of
       -- child_in_law_of: child_in_law_of
       -- parent_in_law_of: parent_in_law_of
       -- sibling_in_law_of: sibling_in_law_of }).applies

  definition subsection equals
    if not taxable_income_applies then Absent
    else if holds_a then Present content Schedule.ScheduleA
    else if holds_b then Present content Schedule.ScheduleB
    else if holds_c then Present content Schedule.ScheduleC
    else if holds_d then Present content Schedule.ScheduleD
    else Absent

  definition holds equals
    match subsection with pattern
    -- Absent: false
    -- Present content sched: true

  definition tax equals
    match subsection with pattern
    -- Absent: $0
    -- Present content sched:
        (output of ScheduleTax with {
           -- sched: sched -- taxable_income: taxable_income }).tax
```
